cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "TLS server certificate chain selection under dual-stack deployment"
message: >-
  If you use these measurements or cite their findings, please cite as below.
  Read PRIOR-ART.md first. It records, per claim, what published work already
  covers, and two framing points here are cited to prior work rather than
  claimed: multiple-chain deployment selected by signature_algorithms_cert is
  described as established practice by Frauenschlaeger and Mottok, who also
  state that OpenSSL already supports it. Three claims were retracted by
  self-audit on 2026-08-10 and one server result is recorded as unresolved.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Danny B."
    family-names: Carr
    name-suffix: Jr.
    affiliation: "Carr Digital LLC"
repository-code: "https://github.com/DannyBCarrJr/pqc-chain-selection"
abstract: >-
  Measured behaviour of TLS 1.3 server software choosing which certificate chain
  to send when the client constrains acceptable certificate signature
  algorithms. The client is a purpose-built probe that emits
  signature_algorithms and signature_algorithms_cert independently, which stock
  tooling cannot do.

  Five server stacks, versions recorded per run rather than assumed: openssl
  s_server 3.5.5, nginx 1.31.3 over OpenSSL 3.5.7, Caddy built from source on
  go1.26.0 over Go crypto/tls, rustls 0.23.43 across three builds (ring, the
  default aws_lc_rs, and rustls-post-quantum 0.2.4 with aws-lc-rs-unstable), and
  Envoy 1.36.9 over BoringSSL. Every cell ships a script and captured output.

  In the headline cell the only configured chain is one the client has excluded,
  an EC leaf key under an ML-DSA-44-signed intermediate offered to a client
  advertising 0x0403 for both extensions. openssl s_server and nginx refuse with
  handshake_failure before sending a certificate. Caddy, rustls, and Envoy serve
  the excluded chain and the handshake completes, so the client validates a chain
  it said it would not accept. The rustls result reproduces across all three
  builds, so it is not an artifact of one cryptographic provider.

  Scope limits are part of the result. The dual-chain and configuration-order
  measurements cover openssl s_server, nginx, Caddy, and rustls. Envoy appears
  on the single-chain cells only: its dual-chain behaviour is recorded as
  UNRESOLVED and withdrawn, because the runner and ad-hoc probes disagreed and
  two of the probe harnesses used to investigate were themselves defective.
  Apache httpd and HAProxy are not covered; both wrap OpenSSL and answer a
  separate question about whether a wrapper overrides its library.

  This is a bench measurement of server software, not a survey of deployed
  sites. Dubey and Varshney (arXiv:2606.16473) found 0% hybrid post-quantum
  certificate adoption across 32,011 domains in 2026, so there is no deployed
  dual-chain population to scan.
keywords:
  - post-quantum cryptography
  - TLS
  - X.509
  - certificate chain selection
  - signature_algorithms_cert
  - ML-DSA
  - public key infrastructure
  - interoperability
license: MIT
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-13"

